WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.449
Have you ever started learning a
language and then a few weeks or months

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:08.250
down the line you just tell yourself, 
that nagging voice of yours keeps

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.219
telling you that there are simply too
many beautiful languages to be learned

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:16.109
in this world? And then you end up
learning many and achieving nothing. Hi

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:19.740
my name is Luca from Luca from LucaLampariello.com and today I'm going to talk about

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:26.200
the diabolical tendency that we all have
to spread ourselves thin and how to deal with it.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.480
The Internet has changed
everything; now whenever we want to, we

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.320
can just go on the internet surf the
internet and find all the resources we

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:43.530
want, if we want to read, write, speak
listen, whatever you want, but the problem

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:49.039
is that that this wealth of information
has actually further exacerbated the

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:53.160
tendency that we all have to spread
ourselves thin - "jack of all trades master

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.809
of none", as they say in English. So, one
thing that has really made a difference

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:03.750
in my journey of learning 14 languages
has been that I stick to one

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.430
language whenever I start learning it. So,
whenever I just set a date, I tell myself

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:13.229
that for at least eight, nine months or
twelve months I will stick to that

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:20.009
language, and my piece of advice is to
create a very strong reminder that you

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:24.420
want to stick to that language no matter
what happens, no matter which "language

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:30.030
sirens" are going to call you, and I have
found out that having post-its on your

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.590
desktop or the place where you learn
every day is great, or also on places

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:39.210
that are visible, such as your
refrigerator -  you can stick a post-its

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.530
post-its are saying "learn just this
language" or maybe just a flag

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.240
representing the language that you
are sticking to, this is really important

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.110
because the temptations are going to be
many, numerous and you don't want to fall

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.000
into the trap of starting to learn
another language.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.470
The reason why sticking to just one
language for a period of time has made a

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:07.580
huge difference is that it allows me to
develop a "language core". A language core

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:14.780
is a an entanglement of many things -
experiences, sounds, words, people - and

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:20.780
obviously it requires time. Imagine for
example dating five girls or five guys

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:26.209
at a time. Sure,  you have a lot of fun but
the reality is that since we have only

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:29.000
so much time
you're literally spreading yourself thin

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.530
you're giving yourself to five people a
little bit and then at the end you

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.290
achieve nothing, you're just dating
people but you're not building a

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.739
relationship and building a relationship
with the language is very similar to

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:42.709
building a relationship with a person: at
the beginning you're a little bit

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:46.310
uncomfortable because you don't know
that person but then it grows on you

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:50.989
with time you get to know that person
you get to know yourself through that

00:02:50.989 --> 00:02:56.090
person the same thing with language and
languages so keep that in mind and think

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:00.200
that you need then you need at least
nine months to twelve months to develop

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.500
a relationship before moving on possibly
to other relationships.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:13.090
Another key reason why sticking to just
one language for a certain period of

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:18.460
time is so effective is that it takes
time to build habits, so not only

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:22.630
building habits in terms of learning but
also in terms of when to learn, how to

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:27.550
learn, your overall approach so it's
important that if you just apply this to

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:32.530
one language it allows you to build
habits for that specific language that

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:37.450
on top of that you can also reuse and
apply to other languages. It's a matter

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:42.400
of time, energy, habits. You have to
remember that the most important secret

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.840
- if there is one secret in language
learning - is learn every day. If you're

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.490
trying to learn multiple languages at
the same time and you are just dabbling in

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.700
many languages, you run the risk of
literally spreading yourself thin,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.080
dispersing your energy and at the end of
the day you won't develop the habits

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.440
that you need to literally "make it
happen", you always have to remind

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.350
yourself the language learning is a
marathon, it takes time, you can't run 40

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:18.000
be 40, be 3, be 2 marathons at the time,
and this is a very important point to keep in mind.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.549
So, here you have it. I hope
you like this video I found it useful

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:25.990
and remember if you want to be serious,
if you are serious about learning

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.400
languages and you want to reach fluency
in one language and move on and learn

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.620
another one, it is really important to
stick to one language at a time and form

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.180
a core especially because the benefits
of speaking a language fluency are immense,

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.200
and once you speak a language fluently,
not only you're going to enjoy the

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.880
process much more, but this allows you to
create the mental and the physical and

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.300
the cognitive space to learn other
languages.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.390
If you want to know about NOT dabbling with languages please click on the link in the description box and

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:03.040
you will be able to read and listen to a
very cool article that my friend and

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.660
colleague Kevin Morehouse has put
together on this fascinating topic.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:11.300
Thank you for listening and see you very very soon here on YouTube

